                    <!-- SIF basics -->
                    <section class="pp-section" id="sif" aria-labelledby="h-sif">
                        <h2 id="h-sif">What Is a Specialized Investment Fund (SIF)?</h2>
                        <p class="pp-answer">A Specialized Investment Fund (SIF) is a SEBI-regulated product offered by mutual fund AMCs. It can use strategies a regular mutual fund cannot, such as long-short positions, and is meant for investors who can invest at least ₹10 lakh.</p>
                        <div class="pp-table-wrap">
                            <table class="pp-table">
                                <caption class="visually-hidden">Key SIF rules tested in NISM V-D</caption>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><th scope="row">Minimum investment</th><td>₹10 lakh across all SIF strategies of the same AMC</td></tr>
                                    <tr><th scope="row">Short exposure</th><td>Unhedged short positions through derivatives up to 25% of net assets</td></tr>
                                    <tr><th scope="row">Strategy types</th><td>Equity-oriented, debt-oriented and hybrid long-short strategies</td></tr>
                                    <tr><th scope="row">Risk disclosure</th><td>5-level risk-band, in place of the mutual fund riskometer</td></tr>
                                    <tr><th scope="row">Who can distribute</th><td>Distributors certified under NISM Series V-D, as per SEBI's July 2026 framework</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </div>
                        <p class="pp-note"><i class="fas fa-info-circle" aria-hidden="true"></i> Because SIFs use futures, options and debt strategies, the V-D exam tests equity and interest rate derivatives along with mutual funds.</p>
                    </section>

